clearQueue: () => void;

/**
Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle — that is, when there are no running or reserved slots (`activeCount` is 0) and the waiting queue is empty (`pendingCount` is 0).

An already idle limiter resolves immediately. Multiple concurrent `onIdle()` calls all resolve when the limiter becomes idle.

A job that rejects still counts as finished, so `onIdle()` resolves once the rejected job settles and nothing else is running or queued.

Note: A job whose result promise never settles keeps a slot active forever, so `onIdle()` will not resolve while such a job is running. Use `clearQueue()` to release the discarded queued promises; when the queue empties and no jobs are active, `onIdle()` waiters are released.

@returns A promise that resolves with `undefined` when the limiter becomes idle.
*/
onIdle: () => Promise<void>;

This is recommended if you await the returned promises, for example with `Promise.all`, so pending tasks do not remain unresolved after `clearQueue()`.

### limit.onIdle()

Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle — both the running/reserved slots and the waiting queue are empty.

An already idle call resolves immediately. Multiple waiters all resolve when the limiter becomes idle. Rejected jobs count as finished. Resolving a job that enqueues another job does not report idle early, and FIFO order and dynamic concurrency changes are preserved.

Note: A job whose result never settles keeps its slot active forever, so `onIdle()` won't resolve while such a job runs. `clearQueue()` releases `onIdle()` waiters when the discarded queue empties and no jobs are active, even with `rejectOnClear: false` where the discarded promises stay unresolved.

```js
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const limit = pLimit(2);
limit(() => something());
await limit.onIdle();
```

